92186283753 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534341 true] [17592186283753 1100 "Find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$  as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x),$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{3}+2x+6.$" 13194139534341 true] [17592186283753 1113 #uuid "4703ff8b-5a2b-5e85-8bf3-fcdffdf5accb" 13194139534341 true] [17592186283753 1145 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=4{x}^{3}+2x+6,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=4{y}^{3}+2y+6\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{3}+2y+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =12{y}^{2}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{12{y}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534341 true] [17592186283754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283755 13194139534341 true] [17592186283754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283756 13194139534341 true] [17592186283754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283757 13194139534341 true] [17592186283754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283758 13194139534341 true] [17592186283754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283759 13194139534341 true] [17592186283754 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534341 true] [17592186283754 1041 "selection" 13194139534341 true] [17592186283754 1104 #edb/ref 17592186283759 13194139534341 true] [17592186283754 1110 #uuid "ce74b2bf-612a-5baa-96d1-bf8cd8afd5c4" 13194139534341 true] [17592186283755 1034 "12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2" 13194139534341 true] [17